X:1
T:Y Cap o Las Fawr
T:The Cap of Broad Lace
R:cân
S:https://amgueddfa.cymru/casgliadau/caneuongwerin/?id=3 (collected by D. Roy Saer)
N:Sung by Miss M. M. Williams (born 1894), a teacher, of Idanfryn, Brynsiencyn, Anglesey; recorded 30.4.59 (St Fagans tape 171). She learnt it as a small child from her father, a Brynsiencyn man, who she believed had picked it up in the "llofft stabal" (the farm servants' loft over the stable) when he was a farm servant. The song turns on Mari's problem: how to pay for her new lace cap without her husband Siôn finding out. The same words were sung at Gaerwen, Anglesey, as part of the song "Cob Malltraeth", and the nonsense refrain ("Rym-ti dwd-li da-am") is typical of folk songs recorded in Anglesey. The museum's English title has "?Broad", as the meaning of "las fawr" is not certain.
N:Each verse is written out on the score with its own rhythms and time signatures, so all three are given here. The slurs on "da-am" join two different notes, as in the source. Each verse's last bar is short, completed by the pickup of the next.
